h all three examples yet.\n\nLet me check columns instead - not sorted either. Trying a different angle: maybe the duplicate pair's position in the output relates to where they appear together in a row, and I need to check the row-major and column-major layouts of the output grid against where repeated values physically cluster in the original grid.\n\nI'm trying to match output rows to specific input cell colors by position, testing whether sorting the cells by column index reproduces the output sequence\u2014checking if row2's 4,5,6 or row1's pattern lines up with column order 0,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9.\n\nSplitting the sorted-by-column values into three groups of three, I notice the output rows match these groups but with the middle group reversed \u2014 this looks like a boustrophedon (snake) fill pattern across the grid, alternating direction row by row. I'm now verifying this pattern against the first example by sorting its cells by column too.\n\n",
